WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.638
Coding Exercise 4 practices conditional aggregation
with PostgreSQL FILTER The

00:00:05.662 --> 00:00:10.738
grain is one row per subject_id so GROUP BY
subject_id is already

00:00:10.762 --> 00:00:14.662
part of the structure You need two separate counts
from the same

00:00:14.675 --> 00:00:19.788
admissions rows For the first metric use COUNT star
FILTER where diagnosis

00:00:19.812 --> 00:00:25.575
equals Pneumonia For the second use the same pattern
with Fracture Give

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.825
the columns descriptive aliases such as
pneumonia_admissions and fracture_admissions The

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.912
benefit of this pattern is that one grouped patient
row can contain

00:00:34.938 --> 00:00:40.625
several condition-specific measures PostgreSQL scans
the same group but applies a different

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:44.962
condition to each aggregate Run and Check then look
at patients who

00:00:44.988 --> 00:00:49.300
have zero for one of the diagnoses Zero is a
meaningful result

00:00:49.325 --> 00:00:52.188
here the patient exists in the grouped data but no
row in

00:00:52.212 --> 00:00:56.612
that group matched that diagnosis condition If you
get a syntax error

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.012
compare the placement of FILTER WHERE parentheses
and quotes with the earlier

00:01:01.038 --> 00:01:06.250
example The main lesson is not only FILTER syntax It
is the

00:01:06.262 --> 00:01:10.400
broader idea of generating multiple metrics from the
same grouped population
